Congress passed an emergency spending  bill last night to keep the federal  government open for two more weeks  it gives lawmakers until December 22nd  to reach a long-term deal that is just  one issue members want to address before  the upcoming holiday break another major  challenge is tax reform next week the  House and Senate may meet to settle  differences between their respective  bills South Dakota Senator John Thune is  one of eight Republican senators named  to the tax reform conference committee  to negotiate the legislation senator  good morning good to have you here it's  nice to be with you let me first ask you  about our new CBS News poll because it  shows that that Americans see these tax  cuts as overwhelmingly benefiting  corporations and the wealthy in fact  nearly two-thirds of Americans thinks  that corporations won't use the tax cut  in order to hire people and create jobs  do you have assurances from big  businesses that they're gonna reinvest  in workers well all the analysis that  we've seen Norah suggests that it may  vary how much they put back into the  business how much they pay out and  distribute dividends or how much they  plow into raising wages and benefits but  it's some amount and if you use 25  percent or 75 percent you get a  different number but all the analysis  that has been done suggests that  reducing the corporate rate the business  rate a lot of that money is going to  flow back into higher wages and so we've  seen as much as $4,000 per household in  this country in terms of the the wage  impact of lowering the business rates  well you've even suggested to  corporations that they could use the tax  cut money to give workers a raise all  workers think that's a really great idea  but we've already heard some  corporations they know they're gonna  pass it on to the shareholders is there  anything that can be done are you trying  to do anything to convince the  corporations you know this might be a  good thing  well I course will encourage it Yael I  don't in a free-market economy obviously  they're gonna make that decision but we  think that if it if they distribute it  in the form you know of dividends to  shareholders that's getting back into  the economy that's gonna benefit a lot  of pension funds a lot of retirement  funds but but we think a lot of it will  go back to workers I mean any company  that sees their tax burden reduced by  that amount is if they want to grow and  expand their operation going to have to  hire more workers and in a competitive  labor market we think that raises wages  for for worker  this country and clearly so many of  these issues are partisan one that's not  is the Children's Health Insurance  Program chip it has bipartisan approval  there's actually a new paper out this  morning in an op-ed in The New York  Times from a bipartisan group of  Governors saying please save kids health  care that fund ran out last September  it's expected to run out nationwide by  early next year if it's not replenished  13,000 children alone in your state are  affected by this 9 million in the  country as a whole  why can't Congress at least come  together when it comes to providing  health care for our youngest and we will  I mean that is something that everybody  agrees upon the chip program is actually  something that got authorized I think my  first year in Congress but it has broad  bipartisan support and my guess is it  will hitch a ride on whatever the year  in spending bill is that we do as you  know it in my state for example the  impact isn't going to be felt to early  next year so we got a little bit of a  window here but I think it'll get done  in the next couple of weeks  a lot of worried families out right let  me ask you this because we did some an  analysis on the median household income  in your state of South Dakota where the  average income is about $50,000 a year  right so that person would pay about 1.4  percent less in taxes the wealthiest  though one percent of South Dakotans  would pay nearly four percent less  that's based on the Senate tax plan so  what would you say to the to the middle  class citizen in South Dakota who says  why are they getting a bigger cut well  if you look at north at you know for  example the Tax Foundation has done an  analysis of an average family in my  state and they've shown that the the tax  cut would be about $2,500 per family but  I just ask people you know sit down and  look at your own tax circumstances plug  in your numbers into your tax return if  you double the standard deduction if you  double the child tax credit if you get  the benefit of the lower rates  you know it's an average family of four  with the combined annual income of  seventy three thousand dollars a year is  going to see a reduction of about twenty  two hundred dollars and that represents  a 60% reduction over what they're paying  under current law so everybody's  circumstances are gonna be different but  if you're on the lower end of the income  scale with that doubling of the standard  deduction I think you're gonna see a  dramatically  and I get you're trying to simplify the  tax code but for what about the people  that are losing the deductions that they  count on state and local taxes medical  expenses the student loan interest I  mean all of those could really hit  people well there will be fewer itemized  errs because of theirs although many of  those deductions that you mentioned are  kept in the Senate version of that bill  so if you want to itemize you can still  take charitable you can still take  mortgage interest you can still take  medical expenses above a certain amount  and so those those could if you choose  to itemize you can still take advantage  of that I think where most people are  going to experience the benefit is with  the reduction in rates I think you're  gonna see a real lowering of rate  particularly you know and if you look at  the Senate bill and how that flows  through the different income categories  but you know everybody's tax  circumstances are different and so I  just encourage people to sit down and  kind of figure out what the the impacts  are for them and their families address  you can address the concerns of adding  to the deficit because they will add to  the deficit over a trillion dollars and  I know the numbers are not final but we  had senator Bernie Sanders here  yesterday I know he's on the opposite  political spectrum of you he's making  the case to that once these tax cuts to  go through that in order to balance the  budget that there will be coming cuts in  Medicare and Social Security can you  guarantee that those cuts won't be made  well I what I would guarantee is that if  we make reforms and we do need to make  reforms to make entitlement programs  sustainable that those are those are  going to be reforms that might for  example say lessen the the rate of  growth and some of those programs over  time but one of the ways that you deal  with the deficit in the debt is you get  a faster growing economy and we've got a  really sluggish economy if we get the  economy growing at even two and a half  percent as opposed to one and a half to  two which is what we've seen for the  last decade then you're gonna get more  government revenue for every one  percentage point increase in GDP it  generates about three trillion dollars  in additional revenue over a decade so  one of the ways to deal with the problem  that Bernie's identified is yes we've  got to restrain spending but we got to  get faster growth and you accomplish  that by reforming our tax code and  making this an attractive place just  turn to the resignation of Al Franken  yesterday he said it's it's not the  irony is not lost on him that the man  who was bragged on tape about his  history of sexual assault now sits in  the Oval Office what do you say to that  now that it looks like  or might be coming to Senate well I mean  I think there are there are men across  all kinds of different industries that  are learning that there are consequences  to bad behavior and I think that's a  good thing you know what happened  yesterday is unfortunate in the Senate  but I think on Capitol Hill the  discussion that we're having right now  is long overdue yes and I think what it  points out is that action is needed you  know we can't continue to accept the  status quo there or anywhere else for  that matter you had called for a Roy  Moore to step aside that is not that  clearly is not the case what will you  all do if he's elected well I think the  one thing I've said all along is if he  comes to Washington he'll immediately be  under an ethics cloud probably an ethics  investigation the result of which could  be recommending expulsion could be  recommending censure there are different  things that the ethics committee  committee can conclude but it's a huge  distraction I mean is the the simplest  wisest thing for him to do in the best  interest of the country and our party is  to step aside and allow another  Republican to get into that race because  I think we would win the seat and yet  the RNC's are supporting him well and  that's an ad that's unfortunate I I  don't condone that I think they made the  right move in the first place all right  very good you could have very good with  you all thank you very good person yeah  person as you do on TV yeah crazy do  [Laughter]

